# Copy this file to .env and fill in BOTH values.
# The .env file is gitignored AND excluded from the image build via
# .dockerignore — it is mounted at runtime with --env-file, never baked in.
#
# NOTE: .env is the *fallback* (Option 2). The preferred path is a Docker
# secret via the Docker Desktop MCP Toolkit (`docker mcp secret set`), which
# injects it as an environment variable. See README.md section 4.
#
# 1) MERAKI_API_KEY — generate in the Meraki Dashboard:
#       My Profile -> API access -> Generate new API key
#    For maximum safety, create a dedicated read-only admin and generate
#    the key under that admin's profile.
#
# 2) MERAKI_ORG_ID — the organization this server instance will serve.
#    Every org-scoped tool is pinned to this ID; the server will refuse to
#    start if it is missing. To find it: list orgs once with the API key
#    (https://api.meraki.com/api/v1/organizations) and copy the `id` value
#    of the org you want this instance pinned to.
#
# Both variables are REQUIRED. The server fails fast on startup if either
# is unset.

MERAKI_API_KEY=your_key_here
MERAKI_ORG_ID=your_org_id_here

# Optional: cap the size (bytes of JSON) of any single tool response.
# Prevents huge endpoints like /events, /traffic, /devices/statuses from
# blowing past the model's context window. The server returns a truncation
# envelope with a `_hint` field when this cap kicks in.
# Defaults to 120000 (~30k tokens). Tune higher for big-window models.
# MERAKI_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=120000
#
# Option 1 (preferred) supplies these same variables from the Docker Desktop
# MCP Toolkit secret store, injected into the environment at launch. The
# server only reads plain environment variables — there is no secret-file mode.
